#  CODEOWNERS

# Owners for the entire repository:
* @paulsinclair51

# Owners for subsets of repository:
# Currently, none.

# Entries:
#   <owns> @<owner> [ @<owner>]...
#
# <owns>: pattern defining a wildcard path. For example:
#   * = everything in the repo (all files, all directories, recursively)
#   /docs/ = just that repo directory and its subdirectories (and files within)
#   /docs/*.md = only .md files directly in repo /docs/ (not nested subdirs)
#   /docs/**/*.md = all .md files in /docs/ and all nested subdirectories
#
# <owner>: GitHub username (account handle/login) or team <org>/<team>.
#
# If patterns overlap (or are duplicated) in CODEOWNERS, the last matching
# pattern wins. So,
#   - Overlap: multiple patterns match same file: GitHub uses the owner(s)
#     from the bottom-most matching rule.
#   - Duplicate same pattern: later duplicate overrides earlier one.
#   - Multiple owners on one line: all owners on that winning line are
#     requested.
#
# Examples:
#   /docs/ @marcusjones
#   /.github/workflows/ @maryjames @britetest/docs-team
#
# Best practices:
#   - CODEOWNERS for this repository is kept at <repo>/.github/CODEOWNERS.
#   - Paths are case-sensitive. Match actual file/directory casing exactly.
#   - Place broad rules first and more specific rules later (last match wins).
#   - Prefer teams for shared ownership (e.g., @org/team) to avoid single-person
#     bottlenecks.
#   - Team owners must be visible and have write access to the repository,
#     or review requests may not work as expected.
#   - Owners are requested for changed files in pull requests; ownership does not
#     by itself block merges unless branch protection/rulesets require approvals
#     and code-owner review. Branch protection/rulesets are in effect for this
#     repository.
#   - Use comments to clarify, but keep active rules minimal and unambiguous.

